const codeThemes = { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' } as const;
const weights = [
  '0.240',
  '−0.180',
  '0.070',
  '0.510',
  '0.304',
  '1.000',
  '−0.420',
  '0.160',
  '0.630',
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Weight Space Mods"
  description="Edit named model tensors with Python, compare evaluations before and after an edit, and save a standalone checkpoint with WSM."
  headings={[
    { depth: 2, slug: 'why-wsm', text: 'Why WSM?' },
    {
      depth: 2,
      slug: 'weight-space-programming',
      text: 'What is weight space programming?',
    },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'how-we-do-it', text: 'How do we do it?' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'why-this-design', text: 'Why this design?' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'whats-done', text: "What's done?" },
  ]}
  source="site/src/pages/index.astro"
>
  <article class="prose landing">
    <section class="landing-hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <div class="identity-line">
        <img src="/brand-mark.svg" width="32" height="32" alt="" />
        <span>Weight Space Mods</span>
      </div>
      <h1 id="hero-title">
        Edit model weights.
        <br />
        Measure the model.
      </h1>
      <p class="lead">
        WSM is a Python SDK for editing model weights. Change named tensors,
        compare evaluations before and after the edit, and save a standalone
        checkpoint.
      </p>
      <div class="landing-actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/docs/quickstart/">
          Start building
        </a>
        <a href="#why-wsm">Why we're building WSM</a>
      </div>
      <p class="runtime-note">
        Python ≥ 3.12 <span>/</span> PyTorch tensors <span>/</span>{' '}
        <a href="https://pypi.org/project/wsm/0.1.0/">wsm 0.1.0 on PyPI</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="why-wsm" aria-labelledby="why-title" class="narrative-section">
      <h2 id="why-title">Why WSM?</h2>
      <p>
        WSM lets you write a weight edit as a Python program, inspect it before
        running, and test the resulting model. You can load the saved checkpoint
        in other tools without WSM.
      </p>
      <p>
        A model exposes several places to intervene. A prompt changes its input.
        A retrieval system changes the information supplied with that input. A
        training procedure changes parameters using an objective and an update
        rule. With direct weight editing, you specify the parameter changes
        yourself.
      </p>
      <p>
        That access matters when your question is about the transformation
        itself. What happens if you scale a particular tensor slice? Can a
        proposed update be restricted to a named layer? Did the exported
        checkpoint preserve the intended edit? These are experimental questions.
        They need a way to inspect weights, execute a precise operation, and
        compare the resulting model against an unchanged baseline.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can already manipulate model parameters with PyTorch. WSM builds on
        that ability and gives the experiment a common execution path. A
        developer or coding agent supplies Python. The runner loads the model,
        measures the baseline, executes the program, measures the candidate, and
        writes a checkpoint with a record of what happened.
      </p>
      <p>
        WSM runs and records these experiments. Finding edits that improve model
        behavior remains a research problem.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section
      id="weight-space-programming"
      aria-labelledby="concept-title"
      class="narrative-section"
    >
      <h2 id="concept-title">What is weight space programming?</h2>
      <p>
        Let <code>θ</code> denote a model's parameters and <code>fθ(x)</code>{' '}
        its computation on an input. A weight-editing program produces a
        candidate parameter set, <code>θ′ = P(θ)</code>. The model you evaluate
        afterward is <code>fθ′</code>. The program specifies a parameter
        transformation. Evaluation determines what that transformation did to
        behavior.
      </p>
      <p>
        We call this <em>weight space programming</em>. The term describes an
        approach, rather than a single algorithm or a feature unique to WSM. A
        program might scale values, add a structured update, combine compatible
        checkpoints, or implement a more involved editing method. In each case,
        code specifies the parameter changes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Weight coordinates are not human-readable instructions. A tensor name
        identifies where a value lives in an implementation; it does not tell
        you which concept that value represents. A small numerical change can
        affect many outputs. A short program can be reproducible without being
        useful, interpretable, or safe to apply to a different model.
      </p>
      <h3>How this relates to existing work</h3>
      <p>
        Fine-tuning also changes weights, usually through repeated optimization
        steps. An explicit editing program and a training procedure are
        therefore related ways to produce new parameters. WSM's current model
        interface exposes detached tensor views and evaluation, rather than a
        differentiable training interface. It does not replace a training
        framework.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685">LoRA</a> learns low-rank
        updates while freezing the pretrained weights.{' '}
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.05262">ROME</a> investigates
        targeted changes to factual associations through rank-one parameter
        updates. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04089">Task arithmetic</a>{' '}
        constructs weight differences between a pretrained model and its
        fine-tuned descendants, then studies operations on those differences.
      </p>
      <p>
        These approaches motivate concrete questions about how parameter changes
        affect behavior. They have different assumptions, objectives, and
        evidence. WSM does not currently ship implementations of those methods,
        and access to tensors does not reproduce their results.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prompting and retrieval remain useful when you need to supply
        instructions or current information. A persistent weight edit changes
        the model artifact itself. Choosing that route means accepting the
        responsibility to measure regressions and preserve the original model
        for comparison.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section
      id="how-we-do-it"
      aria-labelledby="how-title"
      class="narrative-section"
    >
      <h2 id="how-title">How do we do it?</h2>
      <p>
        Start with a local model snapshot and a hypothesis about a parameter
        transformation. The example below scales the first 16 elements of a
        normalization tensor by 0.95. It demonstrates the editing workflow
        without claiming to improve the model.
      </p>
      <p>
        Install the published package with its model dependencies in a Python
        3.12 or later environment.
      </p>
      <Code
        themes={codeThemes}
        code={`python3 -m pip install 'wsm[editing]==0.1.0'`}
        lang="bash"
      />
      <h3>Load, evaluate, modify, evaluate</h3>
      <p>
        <code>WeightModel.load</code> loads a local snapshot without downloading
        model files or executing custom model repository Python.{' '}
        <code>model.weights</code> exposes named tensors. Slicing and in-place
        arithmetic operate on live model storage, so the next evaluation sees
        the modified values.
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <div class="terminal-bar">
          <span>experiment.py</span>
          <span>Python SDK</span>
        </div>
        <Code
          themes={codeThemes}
          code={`from wsm import WeightModel

model = WeightModel.load("/path/to/snapshot", device="cpu")
cases = [{"prompt": "Say hello.", "target": "Hello!"}]

before = model.evaluate(cases)
model.weights["model.norm.weight"][:16].mul_(0.95)
after = model.evaluate(cases)

print({"baseline": before, "candidate": after})
model.save("/path/to/new-checkpoint")`}
          lang="python"
        />
      </div>
      <p>
        The tensor name is specific to compatible model architectures. Inspect
        your model's names and shapes before applying an edit. Tied parameter
        names can share storage, so changing one view can change another name's
        values too.
      </p>
      <figure class="tensor-art">
        <div class="tensor-heading">
          <code>W′ = 0.95 × W</code>
          <span>Illustrative tensor slice</span>
        </div>
        <div class="tensor-scene">
          <div class="tensor-copy">
            <span class="tensor-wordmark">[wsm_]</span>
            <p>
              Named tensors.
              <br />
              Explicit operations.
            </p>
            <code>0.320 × 0.95 = 0.304</code>
          </div>
          <div class="tensor-grid" aria-hidden="true">
            {weights.map((weight, index) => (
              <span
                class:list={['tensor-cell', { 'tensor-selected': index === 4 }]}
              >
                {weight}
              </span>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          The highlighted value illustrates the arithmetic on one element. The
          code scales up to 16 elements of <code>model.norm.weight</code>. These
          values are not an experimental result.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h3>Run the edit as a recorded experiment</h3>
      <p>
        For the CLI, put the mutation in a Python file defining{' '}
        <code>edit(model)</code>. The runner owns the baseline and candidate
        evaluations around that function.
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <div class="terminal-bar">
          <span>edit.py</span>
          <span>CLI entry point</span>
        </div>
        <Code
          themes={codeThemes}
          code={`def edit(model):
    model.weights["model.norm.weight"][:16].mul_(0.95)`}
          lang="python"
        />
      </div>
      <p>
        Create a cases file containing a JSON array of prompt and target
        objects. This one-case example checks the workflow; use cases
        appropriate to your actual hypothesis.
      </p>
      <Code
        themes={codeThemes}
        code={`[{"prompt": "Say hello.", "target": "Hello!"}]`}
        lang="json"
      />
      <p>
        Save it as <code>cases.json</code>, replace the snapshot path, and
        choose an output directory that does not already exist.
      </p>
      <Code
        themes={codeThemes}
        code={`mkdir -p runs
wsm execute edit.py --model /path/to/snapshot \\
  --cases cases.json --output runs/edit --device cpu`}
        lang="bash"
      />
      <dl class="artifacts">
        <div>
          <dt>
            <code>runs/edit/run.json</code>
          </dt>
          <dd>
            Program source hash, execution state, baseline and candidate
            evaluations, and checkpoint location.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>
            <code>runs/edit/checkpoint/</code>
          </dt>
          <dd>
            A standalone model checkpoint with weights and tokenizer files.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <h3>Interpret the result before keeping it</h3>
      <p>
        Evaluation measures target-token negative log likelihood. WSM calculates
        a mean for each case, then an unweighted mean across cases. It does not
        generate a response or apply a chat template. A loss difference answers
        a question about these target tokens under this evaluation procedure.
      </p>
      <p>
        The runner records that difference without automatically accepting or
        rejecting the candidate. Keep development cases separate from held-out
        checks. Test behavior beyond the examples used to design the edit,
        including unrelated tasks you want to preserve. The SDK's public cases
        are visible to the editing program; independent private grading requires
        a separate process.
      </p>
      <p>
        For exact API behavior and execution details, read the{' '}
        <a href="/docs/coding/">Python editing guide</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section
      id="why-this-design"
      aria-labelledby="design-title"
      class="narrative-section"
    >
      <h2 id="design-title">Why this design?</h2>
      <h3>Python expresses the transformation directly</h3>
      <p>
        An edit can use familiar control flow, tensor indexing, and arithmetic.
        We do not require a separate editing language. A coding agent can write
        the same <code>edit(model)</code> file a developer would write, without
        the SDK importing an agent framework. This keeps the program inspectable
        and the execution interface independent of who authored it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Python files run with the invoking process's privileges. The standalone
        runner does not sandbox filesystem access, imports, or other Python
        behavior. The experimental trial system has a separate isolation
        mechanism; it is not a property of ordinary SDK execution.
      </p>
      <h3>Execution and acceptance answer different questions</h3>
      <p>
        The runner can determine whether a program executed and whether its
        checkpoint was published. It cannot infer whether your chosen evaluation
        covers the behavior you care about. A lower development loss alone does
        not establish that the model improved on other tasks.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the same reason, a receipt is a record of an execution, not
        authenticated proof that the program was harmless or the evaluation
        scientifically sufficient. More capable search can overfit an exposed
        metric. Independent grading, regression tasks, and an explicit
        acceptance policy belong in the surrounding experiment.
      </p>
      <h3>The output must survive outside the experiment</h3>
      <p>
        An edit is useful to other tools only if they can load the result.
        Saving writes model weights and tokenizer files, checks finite tensors,
        reloads the staged checkpoint, and compares its tensors before
        publication. The destination is published atomically without overwriting
        an existing checkpoint, on filesystems that support exclusive atomic
        rename.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those checks protect the artifact's integrity. They do not certify its
        model quality. A correctly saved checkpoint can still be a worse model.
        We keep that distinction visible in the{' '}
        <a href="/docs/architecture/">architecture</a> and{' '}
        <a href="/docs/verification/">verification documentation</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section
      id="whats-done"
      aria-labelledby="done-title"
      class="narrative-section"
    >
      <h2 id="done-title">What's done?</h2>
      <p>
        The following implementation and experiment records describe what WSM
        can do and what remains unproven.
      </p>
      <h3>A published SDK and a shared execution path</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="https://pypi.org/project/wsm/0.1.0/">
          Version 0.1.0 is available on PyPI
        </a>
        , with a{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/thoriqakbar0/wsm/releases/tag/v0.1.0">
          matching GitHub release
        </a>
        . The supported public API is <code>WeightModel</code> and{' '}
        <code>execute_program</code>. The CLI and terminal editing action use
        the same program runner. You can load, edit, evaluate, and save without
        adopting an agent framework.
      </p>
      <h3>Tests for edits and checkpoint publication</h3>
      <p>
        The repository's model tests build tiny local models and exercise live
        tensor changes, evaluation, and checkpoint reloads. Failure cases cover
        non-finite weights, existing destinations, interruptions, and execution
        errors. The <a href="/docs/verification/">verification record</a>{' '}
        distinguishes completed runs from tests that require optional
        dependencies. These checks establish implementation behavior, not
        general model improvement.
      </p>
      <h3>A GPU edit and an independent reload path</h3>
      <p>
        The documented{' '}
        <a href="/docs/coding/#reproduce-the-modal-proof">
          Modal demonstration
        </a>{' '}
        checks a deterministic edit on a pinned SmolLM2 snapshot, then checks
        the exported model in a separate CPU environment without the original
        snapshot or model cache. It examines intended tensor changes, unchanged
        tensors, and evaluation parity after reload. This tests the
        edit-and-export mechanism.
      </p>
      <h3>An experimental teacher and student integration</h3>
      <p>
        The retained{' '}
        <a href="/docs/verification/#modal-teacher-and-spindle-student-proof">
          trial verification record
        </a>{' '}
        documents a coding-agent rollout, independent held-out grading on a
        small task, and a response-weighted student training update through
        Modal Spindle. The record reports lower held-out loss on that toy task
        and one confirmed student optimizer update. It does not demonstrate
        improved student editing ability.
      </p>
      <p>
        These results apply to the recorded code and runtime. They do not
        establish reliable autonomous model improvement. Finding useful edits,
        generalizing beyond the evaluated tasks, and repeating gains across
        models remain open research questions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Start with the <a href="/docs/quickstart/">quickstart</a> to run an
        edit, or read the <a href="/docs/research/">research limitations</a>{' '}
        before designing an experiment around the results.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>

